<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy — Sub4
    Patch 191, plan step 2.1.8.

    WHY THE COLLECTED-DATA LIST IS EMPTY, WHICH IS THE ONLY SURPRISING PART

    Apple's definition of "collect" is transmission off the device. Sub4 has
    three paths that could transmit something about the athlete — the AI review
    to Anthropic, the start coordinate to a weather provider, and the Strava
    calls themselves — and every one of them is behind a ReleaseGate.

    `ReleaseGate.permitted` is compiled `#if DEBUG` and is consulted FIRST in
    `ReleaseGates.isOpen`. In a Release build it is false for every gate, so
    `isOpen` returns false unconditionally, `require` throws, and no stored
    value — including one restored from an internal build's backup — can open
    one. A shipping build of Sub4 transmits nothing.

    So this list is empty because that is true, not because nothing was found
    to write in it. THE DAY A GATE SHIPS OPEN, THIS FILE IS WRONG, and
    `PrivacyManifestTests.aShippedOpenGateRequiresDeclaredCollection` fails the
    build to say so. The declarations that would be needed then are written out
    in a comment at the bottom rather than left to be rediscovered.

    ON THE API REASONS

    Two categories are actually touched. Both are declared with the narrowest
    reason that is true, not the broadest that would be accepted.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>

    <!-- No tracking, and no domains to list. Sub4 has no analytics SDK, no
         advertising identifier, and does not combine anything with data from
         other companies' apps or websites. -->
    <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
    <false/>

    <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
    <array/>

    <!-- Empty, and true. See the note at the top of this file. -->
    <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
    <array/>

    <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
    <array>

        <!-- UserDefaults. Twenty-four keys, all of them the app's own: sync
             bookkeeping, schema versions, the release-gate switches, and the
             display settings. Nothing is read from or written to a shared
             suite, an app group, or another app's defaults. -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>CA92.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>

        <!-- File timestamps. ONE use: `AppVersion.built` reads the modification
             date of the app's own executable so Settings can show when this
             build was made. It is displayed to the person holding the phone and
             is never sent anywhere, which is what DDA9.1 describes.

             It is worth knowing this is the whole of it. If the reason ever
             stops being true — because the timestamp starts feeding something
             other than a label on screen — this entry has to change with it. -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>DDA9.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>

    </array>

</dict>
</plist>

<!--
    WHAT THIS FILE MUST SAY ONCE A TRANSFER SHIPS OPEN

    Written down now, while the reasoning is fresh, so that whoever opens a gate
    is not left deriving it. Each entry is `NSPrivacyCollectedDataType` plus
    Linked / Tracking / Purposes.

    Opening `aiReview` — the monthly review to Anthropic:
        NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeFitness           — training load, weekly
                                                      volume, measured paces
        NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUserContent  — the session notes the
                                                      athlete typed
        Linked: false. Tracking: false.
        Purpose: NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality

    Opening `coordinateWeather` — the start coordinate to Apple Weather or
    Open-Meteo, to about a metre:
        NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePreciseLocation
        Linked: false. Tracking: false.
        Purpose: NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality

    Opening the Strava gates does NOT by itself add a collected data type: those
    calls fetch the athlete's own data from a service they authorised, and
    receiving is not collecting. What it does add is a third party with a
    privacy policy of its own, and ADR-0002 is where that lives.
-->
